  "content": "Owen's approach play is behind the team and well behind the Tour. Against the Division I average, he is ahead on short approaches and behind on longer ones.\n\n**Strokes gained on approach:** He is at -4.16 strokes per round over his newest ten-round window, which holds 8 rounds with data (June 9 to September 26). There is no college figure for strokes gained, so there is no level comparison. The team figure is -3.09, and he ranks 6th of 7 players. The Tour baseline is 0.\n\n**Greens in regulation:** He hit 61.11% over 144 holes in that window. That is above the Division I average of 60%. It is below the team figure of 62.73%, where he ranks 5th of 7, and below the Tour's 66%.\n\n**Proximity by distance:** These figures cover all recorded rounds, not the last ten. The team average could not be read for them.\n- **50-125 yd:** 23.77 ft, which is 4.23 ft closer than the Division I average of 28 ft. The Tour figure is 18 ft.\n- **125-175 yd:** 50.58 ft, which is 12.58 ft farther than the Division I average of 38 ft. The Tour figure is 30 ft.\n- **175+ yd:** 94.65 ft, which is 39.65 ft farther than the Division I average of 55 ft. The Tour figure is 45 ft.\n\nHis loss on approach is concentrated in mid and long irons. Wedge distances are not the issue. A practice focus on 125 yd and beyond would target the gap. I can pull his shot patterns from those distances to see where the misses go.",
